// Position matrix (design 235), grid 3 "Graph shapes" — row: 2-cycle.
// `a` imports `b.{beta}` and `b` imports `a.{alpha}`. There is no order in
// which to check them, so the compiler NAMES THE LOOP: `import cycle: a -> b
// -> a`, anchored on the first participating import line and listing each
// edge's line in the hint.
//
// DF-232e: `topological_sort_modules` used to detect the cycle and hand back
// an arbitrary order with the comment "the type checker will handle any
// errors". It did not — a participant was checked before the dependency that
// defines its names, its own check failed, and its export table came out EMPTY
// for everybody downstream, so the error landed on `lib`, a THIRD module that
// merely imports `a` and did nothing wrong, naming a symbol that exists. The
// ERROR-ABSENT line pins that away: the innocent module is never mentioned.
//
// Whether Saw should SUPPORT cycles is a separate, un-asked question. This
// pins that it does not degrade silently.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: import cycle: modules.graphcyc2.a -> modules.graphcyc2.b -> modules.graphcyc2.a
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: undefined function

import modules.graphcyc2.lib

func main() {
    print("{lib.run()}")
}
